-- title:
--   Quadratic omission patterns are exactly completions with two distinct active omissions
-- statement:
--   Let $n\in\mathbb N$, $I=\{0,\ldots,n-1\}$, $E=I\times\{0,1\}$, $B,R\subseteq E$, and $U\subseteq I$. Assume $(s,c)\notin B\cup R$ for every $s\in U$ and $c\in\{0,1\}$. For $t\in I$, let $K=R\sqcup\{0,1\}$ be a disjoint union of index sets, with active-label map $a:K\to E$ given by $a(i)=i$ on the $R$ summand and $a(c)=(t,c)$ on the Boolean summand. Define $\mu:E\to E$ by $\mu(e)=e$ if $e\in R$ or its first coordinate is $t$, and $\mu(s,c)=(s,0)$ otherwise. For $D\subseteq E$, write $\mathbf1_D=\sum_{e\in D}\mathbf e_e$ for its indicator exponent, where $\mathbf e_e$ is the unit exponent at $e$. For a map $f$, let $f_*v$ denote the exponent obtained by summing $v$ over each fiber of $f$. Assume $B\cap R=\varnothing$, $|B|+|R|+|U|=n+1$, and $t\in U$. Let $C=E\setminus(B\cup R\cup(U\times\{0,1\}))$ and $o=\sum_{s\in U\setminus\{t\}}\mathbf e_{(s,0)}$. For an active exponent $d:K\to\mathbb N$ and a state $A\subseteq E$, the omission condition $\mathcal P(d,A)$ means $|A|=n$, $C\subseteq E\setminus A$, every $e\in D=(E\setminus A)\setminus C$ belongs to $R$ or has first coordinate in $U$, and $$\mu_*\mathbf1_D=a_*d+o.$$ For $i,j\in K$ and $\epsilon:I\to\{0,1\}$, define the active completion $$A_{i,j,\epsilon}=B\cup\bigl((R\cup\{(t,0),(t,1)\})\setminus\{a(i),a(j)\}\bigr)\cup\{(s,\epsilon(s)):s\in U\setminus\{t\}\}.$$ For $i,j\in K$ and $A\subseteq E$, $$\mathcal P(\mathbf e_i+\mathbf e_j,A)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad i\ne j\ \text{and}\ \exists\epsilon:I\to\{0,1\},\ A=A_{i,j,\epsilon}.$$
